<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dikwa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dikwa is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. The Sudanese warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> made it his capital after conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> in 1893. After his death in 1900 it became the seat of the Dikwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a>, ruled by a branch of the al-Kanemi dynasty under German and then British control, and it became part of Nigeria in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dikwa.jpg" alt="Dikwa" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr. Snatch · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Round_about_in_Dikwa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dikwa lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, in a region with a semiarid climate, a long dry season and a short wet one, and an annual rainfall of 380 to 810 millimetres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> preferred it to the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> capital because it had better communications and a superior water supply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When Rabih conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem–Bornu Empire</a> in 1893, he destroyed its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukawa">Kukawa</a>, stayed there for a time and then settled on Dikwa, which he fortified heavily and kept as his capital for the rest of his rule. In 1900 he was killed by a French army, reinforced by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> forces, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kousseri">Battle of Kousséri</a>. The al-Kanemi dynasty, which had ruled the empire before his invasion, was restored with French and local support, and with Kukawa in ruins Dikwa remained the capital in the empire's last years under the shehus Sanda Kura, Masta Gumsumi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-garbai">Abubakar Garbai</a>.</p><p>In 1902 all of the empire's lands passed under European colonial rule, and under a treaty of 1893 Dikwa fell under German control. From 1902 to 1916 it was the capital of what the Europeans called German Borno, the present Dikwa Emirate. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the British administered the town and the emirate, first under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> and then under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trusteeship agreement. In 1942 the emirate's capital moved to Bama, though the emirate kept the name Dikwa. A United Nations plebiscite in 1961 made the town and the emirate officially part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dikwa is the headquarters of a local government area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a> with an area of 1,774 square kilometres. Africapolis counted 25,300 inhabitants in 2010, while the 2006 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave an estimate of 105,909 that, as elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, is to be treated with caution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dikwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dikwa</b> had been the capital of Rabih az-Zubayr, the Sudanese warlord who conquered Bornu in 1893.</blockquote><a href="/state/dikwa-emirate">Dikwa Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>He made Muhammad al-Sanusi of Dar al Kuti his vassal, attacked the Kingdom of Baguirmi and in 1893 conquered the ancient Kanem-Bornu Empire, ruling from a new capital at <b>Dikwa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/rabih-az-zubayrs-state">Rabih az-Zubayr's state</a></li></ul></section>
